The == operator in Java compares the references of the objects, not their actual values. This means that if two strings have the same value, but are stored in different memory locations, the == operator will return false. The .equals() method, on the other hand, compares the actual values of the strings. Therefore, using .equals() is the correct approach for string comparison in Java. Here's how you can compare strings:

```java
String str1 = "hacked";
String str2 = "hacked";

if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("The strings are the same.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are different.");
}

if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are the same.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are different.");
}
```

In this example, `str1 == str2` will return false because the references are different, while `str1.equals(str2)` will return true because the actual values are the same.

